(*
env: OEVM_WALLCLOCK_SECONDS=66600
expect:
secs 66600
half 37
finished
*)
(* tuvm#100 — the wall-clock seam: an external RTC (DS3231 on I2C) as the
   timebase, read as SECONDS SINCE LOCAL MIDNIGHT (0..86399).

   The answer shape is deliberately IDENTICAL to ntpSyncTz:'s — an SMI in
   0..86399, or nil. That is the whole point of the seam: a node's schedule
   arithmetic must not be able to tell whether the time came from the network or
   from the RTC, so the two timebases are interchangeable and the strike logic
   proven in 20_bell/05 is reused verbatim. Epoch seconds would not fit an SMI
   (2^30) on a 32-bit target, which is the other reason the day is the unit.

   There is NO tz argument, unlike ntpSyncTz:. NTP hands out UTC and the product
   must apply its own offset; a DS3231 holds whatever local time was written to
   it, so a second offset here would apply the correction twice.

   66600 s = 18:30:00, so half-hour index 37 — the same boundary-exact value
   20_bell/05 pins, which makes the two seams' answers directly comparable.

   On the host OEVM_WALLCLOCK_SECONDS supplies the reading (src/platform.c),
   delivered PER-TEST via the env: header so it cannot leak into 02's
   no-RTC assertion — a global export would turn that test's nil into 66600. *)
class WallClockRead packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | secs |
    secs:: platform wallClock.
    ('secs ' , secs printString) out.
    ('half ' , (secs // 1800) printString) out.
    'finished' out.
  )
) : (
)
